{"id": "19710", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19710", "title": "Limburg (Belgium)", "text": "Limburg is one of the five provinces of Flanders (Belgium). There is also a province called Limburg in The Netherlands. It is the easternmost province of Flanders. The province boasts an interesting mining history, forests and historic towns. The area is mainly visited by tourists from Belgium or its neighbouring countries.\nUnderstand.\nWhereas much of Flanders is built-up, cosmopolitan, and frenetic, the Belgian Limburg is more rural and down-to-earth. \nTalk.\nThe official language, as in all of Flanders, is Dutch, and most people speak it. The main local language of the province is Limburgish which, like Dutch, is part of the Low Frankish language group and is recognised as an endigenous regional language. Another traditional language, Brabantian, is locally used in the nearby regions of Lommel and Tessenderlo.\nEnglish, often learned as a second language at school, is also widely understood especially by younger generations. French, which is spoken in two municipalities, Voeren and Herstappe in the south of the province is often intelligible south of a line from Gingelom to Riemst.\nStay safe.\nBesides a few specific neighborhoods in the larger towns like Genk and Tongeren, the Limburg province is very safe, and tourists do not have to worry."}
{"id": "19711", "revid": "1292031", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19711", "title": "Limburg (Netherlands)", "text": "Limburg is the most southern province of the Netherlands, encompassing the eastern part of the historical region of \"Limburg\" or \"Limbourg\" - the other part forms the namesake province of Belgium. Limburg is different from the rather flat rest of the Netherlands as it is remarkably hilly, it remains predominantly Catholic (while the majority of the country is Protestant).\nThe capital of the Dutch Limburg is Maastricht, famous for its medieval old town and the namesake treaty signed there, which basically created the European Union.\nCities.\nLimburg stretches from south to north over three regions:\nTalk.\nMost people (roughly 78%) in Limburg speak Limburgish in every day life, a regional language closely related to Dutch and German. Dutch is the official language used for practically all forms of official (and most unofficial) written communication. In the larger cities, Dutch is also the main colloquial language. The majority of the people furthermore speak English and German with various levels of fluency. Some French is spoken in areas near the border with Wallonia.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nThe Dutch railway serves Limburg with regional branches and intercity-branches to Amsterdam and The Hague. German railway links connect Heerlen to Aachen and Venlo to M\u00f6nchengladbach by local trains. Maastricht is connected to Liege in Belgium by local train.\nGet around.\n\"Arriva\" operates the local railway concession in Limburg, as well as the numerous regional and municipal bus lines within the province.\nEat.\nA typical Limburg food is the Limburgse vlaai, which is a sweet pie which originally comes from Weert.\nDrink.\nLimburg is famous in the Netherlands for its local beer. The known local brands of beer in Limburg are:\nGo next.\nLimburg is at \"the corner of Europe\", with long borders with Belgium and Germany. You can use it as a starting point to exploring many different regions:"}
{"id": "19716", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19716", "title": "Limerick", "text": "Limerick (\"Luimneach\") is a city in County Limerick in the Shannon Region of Ireland, with a population of about 102,000 in 2022. It's a miniature Dublin with its Georgian streets, historic sights, and many visitor amenities, and is a good base for visiting its surrounding county plus County Clare and County Tipperary.\nUnderstand.\nLimerick grew up at the limit of tides and navigation of the River Shannon. In the 9th century the Vikings first raided it then settled, calling it \"Hlymrekr\" which probably means low-lying and fertile. As elsewhere in Ireland, they were overthrown by the native Irish in the 10th and 11th centuries, who in turn came under the rule of the Anglo-Normans. In 1205 King John ordered the construction of the castle on the river island in the Shannon, and the English settled in this area of King's Island, with their \"Englishtown\" protected by massive walls. South across the small River Abbey lay \"Irishtown\", with the natives firmly put in their place. \nLimerick became a prosperous port, so it was worth fighting for. Some of the many conflicts were the proxy war that was part of the medieval England\u2014Scotland wars; a siege as Oliver Cromwell crushed Royalist opposition in Ireland; and the siege which was the last stand of King James II/VII forces against William of Orange, ended by the Treaty of Limerick in 1691. Supposedly this was signed over the \"Treaty Stone\", which now stands near Thomond Bridge. \nThe city benefited from local agriculture and by growing trade with North America, with Irishtown rebuilt on a grid pattern as a Georgian city comparable to Dublin, and fine public buildings. It slumped with the Napoleonic wars, with the draining of political power to England, and with the great Irish famine of 1847. Not until the mid-20th century did it redevelop, as transatlantic flights began from Shannon and new industry was attracted in to replace bygone trades.\nIt's spawned many great writers and poets: Michael Hogan (1828-1899) the satirical Bard of Thomond, Jim Kemmy (1936-1997) author of \"The Limerick Anthology\", Frank McCourt (1930-2009) author of \"Angela's Ashes\", \"'Tis\" and \"Teacher Man.\" Other famous local people include the actor Richard Harris, TV presenter Terry Wogan, statesmen \u00c9amon de Valera and Michael D Higgins, and physician William Brooke O'Shaughnessy who pioneered IV therapy and the medical use of cannabis. What the place didn't spawn was limerick verse, which evolved in 18th-century England, presumably from a nonsense lyric that referred to the city or county.\nThis is Limerick is the tourist office, within the castle.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\n () is near Shannon town, 25 km west of Limerick along N18. It has flights from Europe and the UK, and from New York, Newark and Chicago. All the main car rental firms have offices at the airport. \nTo town, Bus Eireann 343 runs every 30 min between 8AM and midnight, taking an hour via Bunratty; some may start from Ennis. Bus 51 between Galway, Limerick and Cork also stops at the airport hourly.\nDublin Airport () has a wider selection of flights and is served by Flightlink Bus 723 direct to Limerick, 2 hr 30 min.\nBy train.\nHourly trains from Dublin Heuston take two hours. A few are direct but usually you take the train for Cork and change at Limerick Junction (25 km east of Limerick, near Tipperary); change there also from Waterford. See Irish Rail for timetables, fares and online tickets. \nFive trains a day run from Galway, taking 2 hours via Athenry, Gort and (more frequently) Ennis. \nTwo trains a day M-Sa and one on Sunday run from Ballybrophy on the Dublin-Cork line via Roscrea and Nenagh.\nBy bus.\nDublin Coach M7 (\"Big Green Bus\") runs every 30 min from Dublin Burgh Quay, taking 2 hr 45 min via Heuston railway station, Kildare, Annacotty and UL campus at Castletroy. The last bus departs at midnight. The bus stop in Limerick is Arthur's Quay, M7 doesn't use the bus station. Alternate buses branch here for Bunratty and Ennis, or for Adare, Newcastlewest, Abbeyfeale, Killarney and Tralee. See above for the direct bus from Dublin Airport. \nBus Eireann 51 runs hourly from Cork via Mallow, 2 hours, and continues north to Shannon Airport, Ennis and Galway, 80 min.\nBus 13 runs from Tralee every two hours, and Bus 14 from Killarney and Kerry Airport every two hours, both via Abbeyfeale, Newcastlewest and Adare. \nBus 55 runs every two hours from Waterford, taking 2 hr 30 min via Carrick-on-Suir, Clonmel, Cahir and Tipperary. \nBus 72 runs three times a day from Athlone, taking 2 hours via Birr and Nenagh.\nLimerick bus station is at Colbert next to the railway station.\nBy car.\nFrom Dublin, N7 is all motorway once you get past Naas, 200 km or 3 hours in all. Other major routes from Limerick are N18 / M18 to Shannon Airport, Ennis and Galway, N20 to Mallow and Cork, N21 to Tralee and Killarney, and N24 to Tipperary and Waterford.\nGet around.\nWalking will get you everywhere in the city centre. \nBikes need to be rented weeks in advance. There is only one bike rental shop in town.\nBy bus.\nBus \u00c9ireann operate frequent buses in Limerick. All routes run via city centre:\nTFI website has a map of the city bus network. \nCash fares within the city are \u20ac2.40-2.80 adult and \u20ac1.40-1.70 child, while if paying with a TFI Leap Card are \u20ac1.68-1.96 adult and \u20ac0.98-1.19 child. On TFI Leap Card, 24-hour, 7-day and monthly tickets are also available.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis bear a yellow bar with their licence number and \"taxi\" or \"Tacsa\u00ed\" printed on it. If the light is on, the taxi is available for hire. Ignore unofficial taxis. \nFares are nationally regulated so you pay the same stuck in traffic trying to reach the airport as you do up a back lane in Ballydehob. Taxis are required to use the meter, to accept credit and debit cards as well as cash, and not to surcharge any card. However it's \u20ac3 extra for booking instead of hailing on the street or using a taxi rank, and phone apps may impose a further charge. Fares are revised every two years so from Dec 2024 you pay:\nYou may be able to negotiate a rate for a tour round the sights, eg out to Foynes Flying Boat Museum.\nStay safe.\nCity centre is safe and cosy, even at nights and weekends. Standard advice: beware traffic, safeguard valuables and avoid the occasional drunk. \nThe police station (Gardai) is on Henry Street in the city centre. Their non-emergency number is +353 61 212 400. In emergencies dial the standard 112 or 999.\nConnect.\nAs of 2025, Limerick has 5G from all Irish carriers. Wifi is widely available in public places."}
{"id": "19717", "revid": "2162786", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19717", "title": "Limestone (Maine)", "text": "Limestone is a city in Maine just over the border from New Brunswick, Canada.\nGet in.\nFrom Caribou- Route 89 East.\nFrom North or South- U.S. Route 1.\nFrom Canada- 375 in New Brunswick goes to 229 in U.S. and straight into Limestone.\nGet around.\nThere is never any heavy traffic, not even moderate traffic. So using a car is by far the easiest way to go. The central part of Limestone is very small, walking around town is very easy.\nDrink.\nThe closest nightlife is over the border in Moncton."}
{"id": "19719", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19719", "title": "Limestone (Illinois)", "text": "Limestone is a village in Kankakee County, Chicagoland region of Illinois, United States of America.\nUnderstand.\nThe major industries of the Kankakee Area are health care, higher education, manufacturing, and retail trade. It is the regional retail market center for the area South of the Chicago suburbs, with a strong reputation as a City of Restaurants serving Interstate Highway 57 travelers, from Interstate Highway 57 Exit 308, Interstate Highway 57 Exit 312, Interstate Highway 57 Exit 315, and Interstate Highway 57 Exit 322. Suburban Bradley is the retail hub serving Interstate Highway 57 Exit 315.\nThe Kankakee Area has a hot summer, cold winter climate, typical of the interior Midwest.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nLimestone is located on Illinois State Highways 17 and 113.\nGo next.\nThe city nearest to Limestone is Kankakee, population about 27,000, also in Kankakee County. Nearby villages include Bonfield, Bourbonnais, Bradley, Herscher, and Union Hill, all in Kankakee County. Chebanse is in Kankakee County and neighboring Iroquois County; and Reddick is in Kankakee County and neighboring Livingston County."}
{"id": "19721", "revid": "2248002", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19721", "title": "Limestone Coast", "text": "Limestone Coast is a region in South Australia. It is a tourist region and a wine zone.\nUnderstand.\nHistory.\nFrom the Victoria border to the Younghusband Peninsula this area has been settled since colonisation by mainly European settlers in the 1840s, displacing an indigenous population that had resided in the region for thousands of years. The region supports farming, viticulture, forestry and tourism. Towns include Bordertown, Keith, Millicent, Mount Gambier, Penola, and Naracoorte and the coastal resorts of Beachport, Kingston SE and Robe.\nGeography.\nMuch of the Limestone Coast is low-lying, and was inundated by sea as recently as 2 million years ago. It had previously also been flooded 15\u201320 million years ago. The plains are lined by rows of low sandhills parallel to the coast, created at times when the coastline was at that level. Prior to European settlement, much of the land between the sandhills was swamp fed by streams and subject to inundation. A network of drains totalling 1450 km has been constructed to channel the water away through the sandhills to the ocean. Important areas of wetland remain including the lakes and lagoons such as the southern end of the Coorong and Bool Lagoon. Meanwhile, areas of upland in the Limestone Coast include the volcanic craters of Mount Gambier.\nClimate.\nThe Mediterranean climate of this coast is cool and moist with wet winters. Generally the only times where it may be considered as \"cold\" is during a cold-front which happens quite often in winter.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe primary way to get into the Limestone Coast is by car from the state's capital; Adelaide is via a 4 hour drive South East via the South Eastern Freeway and the Dukes Highway. To get to Mt. Gambier, you'd then have to take the route down south.\nBy bus.\nFrom Adelaide, bus tickets for Adelaide can be purchased at the Sir Robert Helpmann Theatre (cash or cheque only) to Mount Gambier. The bus service from Adelaide is run by Premier Stateliner. It can be just as easy to make a phone booking with Premier Stateliner. Thursdays and Fridays you can get from Adelaide to Mt Gambier at 8:15AM or 5:30PM. Sundays the bus leaves Adelaide at 2:45PM. Other days the service leaves at 8:15AM. The bus trip takes about six hours.\nBy train.\nFrom Melbourne, the V-Line service runs Melbourne to Warrnambool on the train, then from Warrnambool to Mt Gambier as a coach service (it also goes via Portland). V-Line services from Mt Gambier are \"no reserve\", you pay the driver on the day \u2014 bring exact change. V-Line services are generally not busy except during school holidays. They run once a day. From Mount Gambier, the coach does go for a little bit around but apart from going to Mount Gambier, there is no other train stations elsewhere.\nSee.\nThe Limestone Coast is home to South Australia's one and only UNESCO World Heritage Site, Naracoorte Caves National Park full of mammalian fossils which contain the fossils of enormous extinct Australian megafauna such as the marsupial lion, giant kangaroo as well as, which may sound creepy to some; five-metre-long snakes. This was because over the last couple of million years ago, geological processes brought sand and soil into the caves creating thick sedimentary deposits which preserved the remains of the animal living in and around the caves at the time. Up to 20 species of megafauna are preserved at a remarkable level across multiple caves, which all of these make the site is one of the jewels in Australian paleontology.\nDrink.\nCoonawarra.\nCoonawarra, a rural locality on the Limestone Coast, is synonymous with Cabernet Sauvignon, full of plum and blackcurrant fruit, so much so that successes with other grape varieties is overlooked. In the early days Shiraz was the most widely planted grape, and it produces some star wines such as Wynns Coonawarra Estate Michael Shiraz (formerly Michael Hermitage). The limestone geology also suits Chardonnay, Riesling, and Sauvignon blanc.\nCoonawarra has a membership organisation for wineries, the Coonawarra Vignerons Association. Some of the wineries with cellar doors include Wynns Coonawarra Estate. Other wineries who use fruit from the Coonawarra include Yalumba and Penfolds.\nStay safe.\nCrime isn't generally a problem in the Limestone Coast, and following the normal protocols for what you would normally be doing elsewhere would just do you fine. \nAs a relatively homogeneous part of Australia, xenophobia exists in the Limestone Coast, though as a tourist, your chances of encountering overt racism are slim."}
{"id": "19724", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19724", "title": "Limoges (disambiguation)", "text": "There is more than one place called Limoges:"}
{"id": "19725", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19725", "title": "Limoges (Ontario)", "text": "Limoges is a community of 2,000 people (2016) in Prescott-Russell, in Eastern Ontario.\nUnderstand.\nLimoges is one of several communities that make up The Nation township.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom Highway 417 (Trans-Canada Highway), take exit 79 (Limoges Road). Limoges is 2 km to the north; Embrun is a comparable distance to the south.\nSleep.\nMore accommodation is available in Embrun."}
{"id": "19726", "revid": "692", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19726", "title": "Limon", "text": "There is more than one place called Limon or Limone:"}
{"id": "19727", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19727", "title": "Limon (Colorado)", "text": "Limon is in the Eastern Plains of Colorado."}
{"id": "19729", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19729", "title": "Limone sul Garda", "text": "Limone is a town on the edge of Lake Garda. Sandwiched between looming rocky massifs over 1,000 meters high, with stunning views of the mountains above Malcesine on the opposite shore of lake, Limone is one of the most idyllically situated towns on Lake Garda. The narrow, stone-paved streets that wind their way between the high, stone walls of the ancient buildings, and a long promenade, fringed with cafes and restaurants, that culminates in a beach only add to the appeal, making it one of the most popular tourist destinations on the lake.\nUnderstand.\nIts popularity means that Limone is also one of the most touristy places on the lake, which, depending on your perspective, may be a good or a bad thing. It has an abundance of cafes, restaurants and shops selling all manner of souvenirs, food and clothing, and the streets in summer are thronged with tourists. The cafes vie with each other for business and there is often music and even videos blaring from the various establishments.\nThe town was popular with writers such as Goethe, Ibsen and Lawrence, and in 2007, it was used for filming parts of the James Bond film \"Quantum of Solace\".\nOne of Limone's more obscure claims to fame comes from the discovery of ApoA-1 Milano, a mutant form of apolipoprotein, which reduces the risk of cardiovascular disease, and is claimed to be responsible for the high number of people aged over 100 in Limone, more than a dozen for its roughly 1000 inhabitants.\nGet in.\nBy boat.\nFerries[dead link] sail between all the major towns on the lake. \nBy car.\nThe main road from Sal\u00f2 to Riva del Garda goes through Limone.\nSee.\nThe views of the lake, the hills and the town are obvious things to see.\nThere are also the pretty churches of San Benedetto, San Rocco and San Pietro.\nSleep.\nThe official website for Limone, linked at the top of this page, has a fairly comprehensive list of accommodation."}
{"id": "19731", "revid": "1423298", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19731", "title": "Limousin", "text": "Limousin is a region of Nouvelle-Aquitaine, in southwestern France. Because it is off the major transportation routes and has few significant tourist sites, Limousin has attracted little tourism \nTourism is developing, however, because of two major interests:\nUnderstand.\nIt is a rural region with an agricultural (livestock breeding and forestry) and industrial tradition.\nThis region of France is very popular with expatriates from Holland, Belgium and England, with many owning second homes here.\nTalk.\nFrench is the only language widely spoken in this area.\nGet in.\nThere are good road and rail links.\nLimoges international airport receives many flights from Britain and France.\nThere is a project in place to extend the TGV line from Poitiers to Limoges.\nThis region is crossed by the A20 motorway, making it very accessible.\nGet around.\nThere are limited bus routes, therefore a car is required to get around. Cycling is possible especially since the roads are very empty."}
{"id": "19733", "revid": "1811", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19733", "title": "Limpopo", "text": "Limpopo Province is South Africa\u2019s northernmost province. It borders Mozambique, Zimbabwe and Botswana. Limpopo takes up 10.2% of South Africa's land area. Named after the great Limpopo River that flows along its northern border, this province is rich in wildlife, spectacular scenery and a wealth of historical and cultural treasures. \nThe Great North Road from Pretoria was carved by the creaking wheels of ox wagons. Today, when you follow the footsteps of the Voortrekkers, you'll travel on fast, safe roads with modern amenities. Known as the Great North, Limpopo is land of abounding ruins and relics, ancient forests, sparkling trout waters, hot mineral springs and waterfalls. Much of it has remained unchanged for centuries. Limpopo is home to Modjadji, the fabled Rain Queen, the Stone Age and Iron Age relics of Makapansgat Valley and the treasures of Mapungubwe that date back to time immemorial.\nRegions.\nDramatic contrasts in landscape and vegetation are symbolic of the Limpopo Province. Encompassing dry savannah plains, steep valleys and majestic mountain ranges, the province is one of legend and of the early pioneers during the Great Trek. \nThe province used to be known as the Northern Province and includes vast areas of the Kruger National Park and many private reserves. In the North coffee, tea and citrus plantations can be found due to the more exotic climate above the Tropic of Capricorn. The province in divided into four tourism regions; the Capricorn region, the Bushveld Region; the Soutpansberg region and the Valley of the Elephants. \nUnderstand.\nLimpopo borders the following provinces: Mpumalanga, Gauteng, North-West\nClimate.\nThe Limpopo Province enjoys hot yet pleasant summers and dry winters. The weather is characterised by almost year-round sunshine. It can get very hot in summer (October through to March), with temperatures rising to 27 \u00baC and sometimes reaching temperatures in the mid 30s \u00baC. This is mild compared to the Lowveld where some towns such as Phalaborwa have been known to reach 45 \u00baC. One of the great attractions is the Kruger National Park, where tourists can expect summer temperatures around the 30 \u00baC mark. \nTalk.\nSepedi (52%), Xitsonga (22%), Tshivenda (16%). \nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe N1 runs from Cape Town, Bloemfontein and the Gauteng to the Limpopo Province and straight through it and on to Harare in Zimbabwe.\nCar hire and transport services: Public roads in South Africa are well developed and well sign-posted with driving done on the left hand side of the road. Drivers may be required to have an international driver's licence and a minimum of 5 years driving experience to hire a car in South Africa, depending on their nationalities. With a European driver's licence you can drive legally in South Africa for up to 1 year.\nBy plane.\nHoedspruit has an airport with regular flights to Johannesburg.\nPietersburg/Polokwane International Airport is situated five kilometres from town. There is no bus service to town, but major car hire companies have rental kiosks at the airport and taxis are also readily available.\nSee.\nGame reserves.\nWith its vast expanses of bushveld wilderness habituated by an abundance of wildlife species, the Limpopo Province is the perfect destination for nature and wildlife enthusiasts. The Limpopo province is predominantly rural with the result that large areas still remain untouched by human development.\nStay safe.\nTake the same safety precautions as you would in any other international city or town, particularly when sightseeing in built-up urban areas. Avoid unnecessary displays of valuables including jewellery, cash or audio/visual recording equipment. Do not walk in deserted places or go out alone at night. Always lock your car doors and keep the windows closed. It is better to carry travellers' cheques or credit cards than carrying large amounts of cash."}
{"id": "19735", "revid": "2440728", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=19735", "title": "Lincoln", "text": "\"For other places with the same name, see (disambiguation).\"\nLincoln is a city in the East Midlands of England and the county town of Lincolnshire, with a population of 97,541 in 2019. \nIt has a well-preserved medieval centre, with the cathedral, once the tallest building in the world, and castle atop its cobbled Steep Hill. Lincoln High Street is bustling, with numerous independent and chain retailers. \nThe city also has a strong industrial and military history, being the city where the military tank was first invented, and having a lot of Royal Air Force bases close to the city, which is celebrated in the city. \nUnderstand.\nLincoln has a population of approximately 104,000 according to the 2021 UK census, and approximately 128,000 when including villages that effectively act as suburbs of Lincoln. The city sits to the west of the historical county of Lincolnshire, relatively close to the border of Nottinghamshire. \nGeographically, Lincoln is built on the Lincoln Cliff, which runs north to south through the county of Lincolnshire, and creates a natural north-south route through the county avoiding the traditional wetlands. However, a valley runs through the centre of the cliff, with the River Witham, the main river in Lincoln, flowing through this valley. Because of this natural geography, it created an ideal point at which to set up a settlement in the area. \nDue to the cliff, the modern-day city is essentially divided into two, uphill and downhill Lincoln. Uphill Lincoln contains the historical centre, castle and cathedral, whereas downhill Lincoln is newer and was historically more industrial and working-class in flavour. The contrast between the two halves is less noticeable in the 21st century, but there can still be some noticeable differences. \nHistory of Lincoln.\nThe first evidence of settlement in Lincoln was in the Iron Age, approximately the 1st century BCE, around the Brayford Pool area, at the foot of the hill formed by the Lincoln Cliff. Historians have hypothesised that this is where the city gets its name from, with \"lindon\" meaning \"the pool\" in Celtic.\nRoman Lincoln.\nThe Romans conquered Lincoln in 48 CE and built a legionary fortress on the top of the hill formed by the Lincoln Cliff, overlooking the Brayford Pool. The Romans Latinised the name of the city to Lindum. When the legion moved up to York (Roman \"Eboracum\") in 71 CE, the city became a \"colonia\" to secure it against any future invasions. Therefore the name of the city became Lindum Colonia.\nThe River Witham provided an easy access point to the River Trent and then the Humber Estuary, allowing the Brayford Pool to become an important inland port for a time. The Romans also built two important roads through the city: the Fosse Way, providing a route south-west towards modern-day Exeter (Roman \"Isca Dumnoniorum\"), and Ermine Street, connecting modern-day London (Roman \"Londinium\") to York. These routes now form the modern-day A46 and A15 respectively, not straying much from their Roman alignments.\nViking and Anglo-Saxon Lincoln.\nBetween the 5th and 6th centuries, Germanic tribes started to settle in the city. The name then changed to Lincylene, a shrinking of the Latin name.\nAround the 10th century, Lincoln became one of the five East Midland Boroughs of the Danelaw, and became a flourishing city with its own mint. \nNorman conquest and medieval Lincoln.\nThe Nornam conquest of England in 1066 caused Lincoln to be taken over by the Normans. William the Conqueror, the new king of England, ordered Lincoln Castle to be built to secure Ermine Street and the Fosse Way. Lincoln Cathedral was also built just after the conquest to provide a mother church for the newly created \"Diocese of Lincoln\".\nLincoln became a wealthy city in the 1100s, with money flooding in due to exports of cloth and wool. The most famous cloth was \"Lincoln Green\", with its reputation enhanced due to being worn by Robin Hood.\nLincoln had one of the largest Jewish communities in England in the 1100s, but anti-Semitic riots spread to the city, causing the eventual expulsion of the Jews in 1290. The Jews' House sits on Steep Hill, bearing witness to this population. \nLincoln in the Middle Ages.\nLincoln declined in importance in the 14th and 15th centuries as industry moved elsewhere - the Dissolution of the Monasteries cut ecclesiastic income, reducing the city's fortunes further.\nIn 1549, the great spire of Lincoln Cathedral collapsed and was not repaired, symbolising the city's misfortunes at the time.\nIn addition, during the English Civil War, many buildings became badly damaged, and the easy access the city had enjoyed to the sea was lost, causing many residents to flee by the 1700s.\nGeorgian and Victorian Lincoln.\nThe Agricultural Revolution changed Lincoln's, and indeed Lincolnshire's, fortunes significantly. Lincolnshire became the \"bread basket\" of England, and this new wealth flooded into the city. A significant expansion of the city happened during this period, with new areas such as the West End being built.\nIn the 1840s, the railways came to the city, with two competing lines being built - the Midland Railway opening Lincoln St. Marks station, and the Great Northern Railway opening Lincoln Central station. \nHeavy industry also developed during the 19th century, with companies such as Ruston's, Robey's and Willam Foster's building engines for agricultural and railway applications. This caused Lincoln to grow significantly, with people moving to the city from the countryside and new housing being built around the city centre.\nThe railways were concentrated downhill, around the River Witham, meaning that heavy industry was concentrated there as well. The Great Northern Railway chose not to build their primary route through the city due to protests from Lincolnshire aristocracy, and the Great North Route (the modern day A1) was also built towards the west of the city to avoid the Humber. \nThis meant that the medieval centre never saw major re-development, and remains one of the most attractive in Britain. \nModern day Lincoln.\nLincoln in the 21st century is a prosperous and lively city, less reliant on industry and more on services, with many amenities offered by larger cities but also having a small-town feel. The two local universities, the University of Lincoln and Lincoln Bishop University, have brought with them a more youthful feel to the city, and regeneration of the downhill city centre has made it a much more attractive place to visit. \nCompared to other similar-sized cathedral cities, such as York or Durham, Lincoln is a little off the beaten track on the UK's rail and road networks so it sees fewer visitors. It loses none of its appeal for that, and you can explore the cathedral, castle and historic streets without the crowds. \nWhen to visit.\nThe most popular time to visit Lincoln is in the summer, when the weather is best and all attractions are open. Summer is also the busiest time in the city's event calendar.\nHowever, autumn and winter also offer good opportunities to visit, with a lot going on around the city and a chance to explore the city more easily at night.\nSpring is also a good time to visit, as the surrounding countryside comes alive and is a popular time to visit the city's parks.\nVisitor information.\n is at 9 Castle Hill at the crossroads with Bailgate and the cathedral precinct's Exchequer Gate. It's open M-Sa 10AM-5PM, Su 11AM-4PM, tel +44 1522 545458.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nLincoln does not have its own airport but it is relatively easy to get to and from many UK airports.\nBy train.\nThe primary railway station is, formerly called Lincoln Central railway station. It is situated in the city centre on St Mary's Street and is therefore close to all major attractions. Facilities include a staffed ticket office, ticket vending machines, an ATM, toilets, and a Costa Coffee shop.\nIt is possible to buy a Plusbus ticket which gives unlimited travel around the Lincoln area for the validity period of the ticket.\nThe primary intercity train operator is LNER[dead link] who operate trains every two hours between London Kings Cross and Lincoln via Peterborough, Grantham and Newark Northgate.\nRegional trains are mostly operated by East Midlands Railway with regular service towards Newark Castle, Nottingham, Crewe, Matlock, Doncaster, Grimsby, Cleethorpes and Peterborough.\nNorthern Railway also operate regional services to Sheffield via Gainsborough, Retford and Worksop, with many of these continuing to Leeds via Barnsley.\n is the secondary station in Lincoln, mainly serving the southwestern parts of the city, as well as North/South Hykeham. However, services and facilities are much more limited.\nBy bus.\nThe main bus station in Lincoln is, which is located across from the railway station. It is a modern building, with a covered waiting area and good signage, as well as a cafe and paid public toilets.\nNational Express is the only long-distance coach operator operating to/from Lincoln, with two return services a day:\nIt is possible to use the 151 to get to London with a change in Leicester.\nRegional buses converge on the city from across Lincolnshire, for instance from Grimsby, Market Rasen, Louth, Skegness, Horncastle, Sleaford, Grantham, Gainsborough and Scunthorpe.\nBy road.\nFrom the south, take the A1 north to Newark, and then the A46 north to Lincoln. This becomes the Lincoln bypass and continues towards Grimsby. If you are using the M1, leave at junction 21A and join the A46 near Leicester.\nFrom the south-east via Peterborough and Sleaford, take the A15, the Roman \"Ermine Street\", towards Lincoln. It forms the eastern section of the Lincoln bypass and then continues north towards Scunthorpe, the Humber Bridge and Hull. On the sections approaching Scunthorpe, be very careful, as the road is single-carriageway and has many drivers attempting dodgy overtakes.\nFrom the north, one route is to join the A1 southbound until Markham Moor (look out for the Lincoln signs and the Starbucks with a parabolic roof, designed by Lincoln architect Sam Scorer). Then, use the A57 eastbound to get to Lincoln. Dunham toll bridge is on the border between Lincolnshire and Nottinghamshire, with the toll charge for a car being 70p as of September 2026. Cash is preferred, as card payments often take a very long while.\nAnother route from the north is to use the M18 and then join the M180, Lincolnshire's only motorway, eastbound until it meets the A15.\nParking.\nMost car parks in the city are operated by the City of Lincoln Council. The largest is, which is at the end of the A57 near the railway station, and is close to the major attractions.\nFor the uphill area, around the Cathedral and Castle, is the largest, next to the west gate of the Castle. Otherwise, the Westgate 1 and 2 car parks are only a short drive away.\nIf you are doing a day trip into Lincoln, you may want to consider the which departs from the north-east of the city.\nStreet parking is limited around Lincoln, and many of the residential streets around the city centre now operate a residents permit scheme.\nGet around.\nLincoln city centre is very compact, so lends itself well to walking. The medieval spine of the city centre isn't suitable for anything more elaborate than a pack-mule or hand-cart.\nHowever, beware of Steep Hill - it is called Steep Hill for a reason and can be a challenge for some people, for example if you are unfit or less able.\nBy bus.\nThe main bus operators within Lincoln are Stagecoach and PC Coaches. \nMost of Lincoln's buses follow a radial path, with buses starting or terminating at Lincoln Central bus station. \nThe most up-to-date bus information is given on the LincsBus.com website, and a route planner is available through Citymapper. \nStagecoach publish a map with the key routes and points of interest identified. \nThe Walk &amp; Ride bus performs a circular route connecting the lower part of the High Street to the Castle and Cathedral Quarter. It operates approximately every 20 minutes Monday-Saturday (not including bank holidays). \nBy car.\nIt is not recommended to drive within Lincoln city centre if you can help it - it is very compact and walkable, and the road layout, especially in the uphill area, is very confusing even for locals.\nHowever, you may want to rent a car to explore more of Lincolnshire, and Lincoln has branches of Enterprise and Switch Car Rental. A couple of local companies are Feathers Rental and Ready Rent a Car.\nBy taxi.\nThe main taxi operators are Handsome Cabs (\u260f), Direct Cars (\u260f) and A2B Cars (\u260f). Uber also operates in Lincoln. There is a large taxi rank outside the railway station.\nBy cycle.\nLincoln has several cycle paths within the city, similar to other cities/towns within the UK.\nSee.\nLincoln has a lot of attractions for a city of its size - of course the most well known are the Cathedral and Castle, but there is plenty to discover in the city besides.\nDo.\nSports.\nAs a spectator.\nThe main spectator sport in Lincoln is football, with the main team being \nBuy.\nLincoln has the usual range of high-street chains, mostly found downhill, but also has a good range of independent shops, mostly found uphill. \nEat.\nLocal specialities.\nLincolnshire is a predominantly agricultural county, and this is reflected in the speciality food within Lincoln itself - many residents are very proud of the local produce, and it is very easy to find at local restaurants.\nSome of these include Lincolnshire sausages, which are well-known across the UK, and differ from other British sausage types by using coarser-ground pork and a heavy use of sage for flavouring. Locally-grown fruit and vegetables, especially root vegetables, are widely available. Other specialities include haslet, which is a pork meatloaf flavoured with herbs, and Lincolnshire Poacher cheese.\nEating out.\nLincoln has a surprisingly good food scene for a city of its size, with varied outlets to suit all budgets. As well as the typical chains found in a lot of UK cities, there are numerous independent offerings across the city.\nDrink.\nLincoln has a wide variety of pubs and bars throughout the city, many of which serve local ales. The student population ensures a healthy nightlife scene, but perhaps not as good as Nottingham's or Sheffield's, both of which have more variety.\nPubs.\nMost pubs in Lincoln are along the High Street or just off the High Street, with many of these growing with the city and having a long history.\nSleep.\nLincoln has a reasonable choice of hotels and other accommodation, most of them mid-range. Compared with cities like York, there's less at either end of the scale, and the city has long had fewer hotel rooms than its visitor numbers would suggest, so book ahead for busy weekends and big events at the Lincolnshire Showground.\nStay safe.\nLincoln is a very safe city for visitors, and the historical uphill area is calm at almost any hour. Most problems are the usual ones for a small British city with a large student population, and there are a couple of hazards that are uniquely Lincoln-related.\nIn an emergency, you can call 999 or 112 as with the rest of the UK. For non-urgent police matters, you can call 101, but this may not work on non-UK mobile phones.\nNightlife.\nWeekend evenings, and student nights during term, can get rowdy around the Brayford Pool, Silver Street and the lower High Street. Trouble is rarely aimed at visitors. If someone who has had too much to drink tries to pick a fight or pester you, keep walking. The uphill pubs around Bailgate and the castle are usually a quieter bet. Lincoln also gets its share of hen and stag parties, and pubs, especially along the lower High Street, get very busy on Lincoln City FC home match days.\nSteep Hill.\nThe name is not a joke. The cobbles on Steep Hill and The Strait are polished smooth by centuries of feet and become treacherous in rain, frost or ice, especially going downhill. Make sure to wear shoes with grip, use the handrails, and especially be careful descending Steep Hill after a heavy session in the uphill pubs. If you're less mobile, the Walk &amp; Ride bus avoids the climb entirely.\nWater.\nBrayford Pool and the River Witham look placid, but the water is deep, cold and has sheer edges with few ways to climb out. Unfortunately there have been tragic incidents where people have been injured, sometimes fatally, after falling into the Brayford Pool, especially after drinking or taking drugs. \nLevel crossings.\nThe railway cuts straight across the High Street and Brayford Wharf East near Lincoln Central station. During busy times, the barriers can stay down for minutes at a time. Wait or use the footbridges. Don't be tempted to climb over the barriers, as trains can often come faster than anticipated.\nStay healthy.\nAs with all of the UK, the numbers to call for a medical emergency are 999 or 112.\n111 can be called for non-emergency NHS medical help, but may not work on non-UK mobile phones.\nConnect.\nLincoln has 5G from all UK carriers. \nGo next.\nLincoln is near the centre of Lincolnshire, and close to much of Nottinghamshire as well, so makes the ideal place to explore much of the eastern part of the East Midlands."}
